<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean-François de La Rocque de Roberval</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500–1560</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean-François de La Rocque de Roberval was a French nobleman and Protestant courtier whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-duke-of-nevers">Francis I</a> appointed in 1541 as lieutenant-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to found a colony. Arriving in 1542, he wintered with his settlers at Cap-Rouge near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> City, where many died of scurvy, and the colony was abandoned in 1543. He was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1560.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-francois-de-la-rocque-de-roberval.jpg" alt="Jean-François de La Rocque de Roberval" width="291" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Clouet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois_de_la_Roque_de_Roberval.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
